title: "The Cotton Line: Is an Erased Colonial Land-Revenue Boundary Still Visible in Vidarbha's Satellite and Census Record?"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/THE-COTTON-LINE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
    description: "Zenodo archive (earlier release; to be updated for v2.1)"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22111298"
abstract: >-
  Tests whether the former boundary between Berar (Ryotwari land revenue) and the Central
  Provinces (Malguzari), abolished in 1956 and proxied by the 384.7 km Amravati/Nagpur Division
  line, is detectable as a spatial discontinuity in present-day Vidarbha, India. Outcomes are a
  Landsat NDVI cropping-intensity index (November to March, two years), Landsat land surface
  temperature, and Census 2011 labour structure and net sown area, at three scales (32-taluka
  corridor, 98 Landsat talukas with 30 m cloud- and cropland-masked NDVI, 119 census talukas). A spatial-discontinuity regression with
  rainfall and elevation controls, conservative HC3/Conley inference and Holm correction over 20
  tests finds no level step at the line. The NDVI index peaks near the line on both sides and
  falls away in both directions; slope differences at the line reflect that ridge, and placebo
  lines reproduce them for NDVI and labour. The index tracks district cotton share (r = 0.86).
  The cotton core straddles the old line; there is no boundary effect. Full pipeline in Python (numpy/pandas/scipy/tifffile).
keywords:
  - spatial discontinuity
  - boundary persistence
  - historical GIS
  - Landsat
  - NDVI
  - land surface temperature
  - Conley standard errors
  - Vidarbha
  - India
